Key findings:
- Insertion sort is always slower than builtin sorted() (Timsort)
- The slowdown varies dramatically by input order:
- Sorted input: 6-15x slower (best case for insertion sort: O(n))
- Reverse sorted: 75-2235x slower (worst case for insertion sort: O(n²))
- Random/duplicates: 28-279x slower (O(n²) average)
- The ratio grows with size, especially for reverse-sorted (confirming O(n²) vs O(n log n))
- At size 512 reverse, insertion sort is 2235x slower!

Critic concerns to address:
1. Timing bias: We randomized measurement order, used time.perf_counter(), and did 50 reps
