3. Edge cases: Tested empty, singleton, negatives, duplicates - all pass
4. Misleading speedups: None claimed - insertion sort is always slower
5. Repeated measurements ≠ independent samples: Acknowledged in report

Follow-up hypothesis: The builtin sorted() uses Timsort which has adaptive behavior. For already-sorted data, Timsort should be O(n) too. Let me test whether the builtin also benefits from presortedness, and whether the ratio between sorted and reverse inputs is consistent with Timsort's adaptive behavior.

Actually, a better follow-up: test whether the insertion sort's O(n) best case (sorted input) is genuinely O(n) vs O(n²) worst case (reverse). We can do this by testing more sizes and checking the scaling.

Or: test with larger sizes to see the crossover point where insertion sort becomes impractical.

